\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Generic U-Net topology \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ronneberger_u-net_2015}: a contracting path (left) halves the resolution and doubles the channel count $C$ at every level, a bottleneck (centre) holds the coarsest, most contextual representation, and an expanding path (right) mirrors the contraction. Skip connections (dashed) concatenate each encoder level's feature map into the decoder level of equal resolution, so fine spatial detail bypasses the bottleneck. The concrete instantiation used by the correction pipelines ($C=32$, four levels, FiLM conditioning) is described in Sec.~\ref {subsec:pipe-unet-flow}.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:unet_generic}{{1.4}{11}{}{figure.1.4}{}}
\newlabel{eq:flow_matching}{{1.7}{11}{}{equation.1.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:linear_interpolant}{{1.8}{11}{}{equation.1.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{Diffusion}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sssec:diffusion-theory}{{1.2.3}{11}{}{figure.1.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces The straight-line interpolant of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:linear_interpolant} used for conditional flow matching \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {lipman_flow_2023}: training regresses the network's velocity $v_\theta (x_t, t)$ onto the constant per-sample velocity $u_t = x_1 - x_0$; sampling integrates $\dot  {x}=v_\theta $ from $t=0$ to $t=1$. When $x_0$ is chosen close to $x_1$ -- as in Sec.~\ref {subsec:pipe-unet-flow}, where $x_0$ is the low-fidelity map itself rather than noise -- the trajectory is nearly straight and a handful of Euler steps suffice.}}{12}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flow_interp}{{1.5}{12}{}{figure.1.5}{}}
