\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Full-sky reconstruction from a patch model (\texttt  {ml/analysis/patch\_tiling.py}). Top: tiles are extracted by cached gnomonic index maps, corrected in batches, and blended with a radial cosine taper $w$ raised to a power $p$ before normalisation. Bottom: the weight profiles of three neighbouring tiles along a cut through the sky. At $p=1$ the profiles overlap broadly, so each pixel is an average over $N_{\mathrm  {eff}}$ tile predictions -- harmless for the deterministic flow, whose overlapping predictions agree, but destructive for a stochastic sampler, whose independent samples average out. At $p\gg 1$ each profile collapses onto its tile centre and every pixel is dominated by a single prediction, so the overlap provides only context, in the spirit of the original overlap-tile strategy \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {ronneberger_u-net_2015}.}}{21}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tiling}{{2.6}{21}{}{figure.2.6}{}}
